$$

questions.answer:
$-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{6}$
分子有理化：
$$\frac{\sqrt{3-x}-\sqrt{1+x}}{(x-1)(x+2)}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{3-x}+\sqrt{1+x}}{\sqrt{3-x}+\sqrt{1+x}}
=\frac{(3-x)-(1+x)}{(x-1)(x+2)(\sqrt{3-x}+\sqrt{1+x})}
=\frac{-2}{(x-1)(x+2)(\sqrt{3-x}+\sqrt{1+x})}$$
约去 $(x-1)$ 后代入 $x=1$：原式 $=\dfrac{-2}{3(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{2})}=-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{6}$。

$\dfrac{\pi}{3}$
令 $t=\sqrt{x-2}$，则 $x=t^{2}+2$，$\mathrm{d}x=2t\,\mathrm{d}t$，$x+7=t^{2}9$：
$$\text{原式}=\int_{0}^{+\infty}\frac{2t\,\mathrm{d}t}{(t^{2}+9)\cdot t}
=2\int_{0}^{+\infty}\frac{\mathrm{d}t}{t^{2}+9}
=2\cdot\frac{1}{3}\left[\arctan\frac{t}{3}\right]_{0}^{+\infty}
=\frac{2}{3}\cdot\frac{\pi}{2}=\frac{\pi}{3}.$$

$\frac{\pi}{4}$

令 $u=\frac1x$，则 $\mathrm{d}x=-\frac{1}{u^2}\,\mathrm{d}u$。当 $x=1$ 时 $u=1$，当 $x\to+\infty$ 时 $u\to0$，故
$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{1}^{+\infty}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{x\sqrt{2x^2-1}}
=\int_{1}^{0}\frac{-\mathrm{d}u}{\sqrt{2-u^2}}\\
&=\int_{0}^{1}\frac{\mathrm{d}u}{\sqrt{2-u^2}}
=\left.\arcsin\frac{u}{\sqrt2}\right|_{0}^{1}
=\frac{\pi}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

